Z||000000||Jncomutt||11-28-2001||07:32 PM||||I've read alot about changing to the blazer master cyl. when you do the brake upgrades.  My question is whether or not I can hook up the blazer MC to my stock brake system.  I was readin somehitn about IRM and noticed they made/make a big bore MC and that it helps stopping distances.  <P>Was wondering about similar effects...||||reg||1
Z||000001||jelly2m8||11-28-2001||10:19 PM||||When I installed the GA brakes at the 4 corners of my one car, I went with the stock Fiero MC. I did buy the Blazer MC at the same time, so I did try that after a few runs with the Fiero MC.<P> I did not notice any difference in pedal heigth, or the way the brakes perform between the blazer MC and the stock Fiero one.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-28-2001||11:33 PM||||A blazer, or any other larger diameter MC will ALWAYS REDUCE braking power at an axle using OE Fiero parts.<P>Actually, compaired to the Fiero MC it reduces power with grand am parts as well.<P>why is explained in my cave.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jncomutt||11-28-2001||11:44 PM||||so it will be safe and better the braking in my 84 to switch to a blazer MC??<P>I'm thinking about it, and I have one available.  Just lookin to see if I should first...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||11-29-2001||07:39 AM||||Using the Blazer MC will make your brakes worse.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pyric||11-29-2001||08:29 AM||||Yeah, Ogre's cave has a whole article about possible brake upgrades and their consequences.  The safest thing is to keep the stock system unless you are absolutely positive of what you're doing.  <P>If you're going to do anything, get stainless steel brake lines.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||11-29-2001||01:11 PM||||For smaller fiero brakes and grand am brakes perhaps the Blazer will hinder stopping power. But when I did my 11.25" conversion and tried the Fiero MC , it sucked , the pedal was mushy and it was hard to vary stopping pressure before lockup.<P>I think there is a certian transition size between rotor , MC , and calipers. I know as soon as I put the the Blazer unit I noticed quite an improvement in pedal feel and stopping distances.<P>Hope this helps<BR>JM <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000007||MinnGreenGT||11-29-2001||01:26 PM||||Well, I have the GA brakes on my car- and I'm still running the Fiero MC.  I can't really compare braking power from the stock Fiero setup to the GA setup- the brakes were pretty well shot when I replaced them, and anything would've been better.  Anyway- I'll probably try the Blazer MC next year.  And I picked up a G-Tech so I'll run some 60-0 times before & after... but not 'till next year so don't hold your breath.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||11-30-2001||08:58 PM||||I used the Blazer MC on my car because it and the calipers I'm also using have almost the exact same proportionate volumes,(cylinder sizes) as the stock parts did.<BR>Oops,make that Blazer diesel for the near-exact match,but I used the regular Blazer to get an advantage in the pedal pressure;works fine so far,but have never taken it to the track either.<BR>I can't imagine that the Grand Am parts are really that much larger than stock,'84-'87 Fiero parts,maybe a little.||||reg||1
