Z||000011||theogre||12-01-2001||12:41 AM||||There is a downloadable Excel sheet in my cave. It covers nearly all the combinations for FS blazer and OE MC with OE calipers and GA calipers. (I locked the sheet to prevent accidental changes. There isn't a password on the spread sheet. just unlock it.)<P>There is enough info there that, ignoring the vacuum power assist, you can work out exactly what pressures you'll get in the calipers for a given combination. That spread sheet can also be used to calc any MC caliper you can think of. Just take one of the exisiting combinations and enter the MC and caliper piston diameters. (Power assist is a constant so you can effectivly ignore it in the calculations. You'll end up with the esentially the same numbers for a comparison.)<P>Adding a FS Blazer MC to a Fiero with any OE calipers DOES NOT help performance of OE parts. It makes them perform worse.<P>Slammed, <P>the most important relation ship is MC to caliper volume. Your calipers must be using more volume than the OE Fiero MC can deliver properly. You were probably squeezing one or both circuits nearly dry.<P>The other issue is caliper pressure. Even if the MC was able to deliver the fluid volume you needed, you may have been over pressure at the caliper for the weight of the car. adding a larger bore MC reduces the line pressure and thus caliper pressure.<P>In short, yes, there's a sweat spot in the ratio of the two parts.<P>All,<P>Properly balanced braking is like a dance. All the parts have to work to the music or you will have problems. The odds are that those prolems may not show up until an emergency stop. <P>I'm not sorry. I don't trust anyone that advocates an illegal upgrade for street use. As it exists, the 4 wheel GA/Blazer upgrade is and always will be illegal for street use. It doesn't matter is you can get thru inspection or you state doesn't have inspection. The upgrade is federally illegal because it doesn't meet FMVSS 135 reequirements.<P>All the brake material in my cave is a direct result of GA/Blazer upgrade being to good to be true. All of it. Including the fluid and all service material.<P>I've been working on cars most of my life. Easily 30 years now. I've spent allot of time learning about brakes. They are one of the most intricate and delicate systems in a car. It is extremely easy to F them up. It is just as dificult to get them right.<P>I've spent the better part of 2 years now working on the issues of finding a decent brake upgrade that won't bankrupt people. Which is way more than Fiero Store... We've seen how they do research. Hmm... Rodney has a neat part.... Let's knock it off. Do you really think they did any better on the brake packages?<P>I've been thru more brake documentation in that time than most people ever dreamed existed. If you want to have some real fun... go visit <A HREF="http://www.uspto.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.uspto.gov</A>  and learn how to find and read the patents on various parts of Fiero's brakes. I spent over a month on just that. A GM patent is where my cutaway view of the rear caliper originally came from.<P>In that time and material, I've learned more about brakes than I ever thought I could or wanted too.<P>People who really understand brakes, like Baer and Willwood, would never sell you a system like the GA/Blazer package. Not even as a front only kit.<P>There are several people here in the forums who have tried the GA/blazer parts and didn't like them.<P>Many people make assumptions about the Fiero brake system that simply don't hold water. The assumption that a solid rotor is automatically bad is one of those. Solid rotors, while rare on front are near universal on rear. Even very heavy SUVs like Ford Explorer and Jeep Grand Cherokee use them in rear. Those vehicles out weigh Fiero by at least twice and can carry most of a Fiero's weight as cargo. I've even seen them on Porsche and other very expensive, high performance, cars. And many Porsche models are rear engine cars.<P>Yes, there are issues with Fiero OE parts in performance applications. No, Throwing parts at it is not a good fix.<P>I still think there's a good fix out there. I don't think it's nearly as simple as the GA/blazer parts pitching.<P>Just for the record....<P>I did start buying parts for the front GA upgrade at one point. It was when I saw how poorly the GA rotors fit that I started digging. I could not believe that anyone would actually center a rotor to the non machined inner diameter of the hat. That just about guarantees that the rotor will be off center. Even small imbalances are a problem. (Even if you can't feel them in the seat of your pants or the steering wheel.)||||reg||9
